{"passages": [{"reference": "2.25", "text": "Simon, however, took Helen with him and travels about everywhere, and to this very day—as you see—he throws the crowds into turmoil. He says that he has brought Helen herself down from the highest heavens to the world, she being mistress, as an all-mother essence and wisdom. For her sake, he says, Greeks and barbarians fought, having imagined an image of the reality; for she who truly existed was then with the very first God. Nevertheless, by giving a plausible allegorical interpretation to certain such things concocted together with Greek myths, he deceives many, especially by performing many monstrous wonders. If we did not know that he does these things by magic, we ourselves would also have been deceived. But since we were his associates at first, when in doing such things he did not harm the cause of reverence toward God, we have now left him, when, having gone far into madness, he began trying to deceive those who live in reverence toward God.", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "ἐκπεριέρχεται", "chosen": "travels about everywhere", "rationale": "The compound describes Simon’s continued movement from place to place."}, {"source_quote": "κυρίαν οὖσαν, ὡς παμμήτορα οὐσίαν καὶ σοφίαν", "chosen": "she being mistress, as an all-mother essence and wisdom", "rationale": "This retains the predication of sovereignty and the unusual description of Helen as both universal maternal essence and wisdom."}, {"source_quote": "εἰκόνα φαντασθέντες ἀληθείας", "chosen": "having imagined an image of the reality", "rationale": "The contrast is between the image over which people fought and the real Helen said to be with God. “An image of truth” is also lexically possible."}, {"source_quote": "πιθανῶς ἀλληγορῶν", "chosen": "giving a plausible allegorical interpretation", "rationale": "πιθανῶς may describe the interpretation as plausible or Simon’s manner as persuasive; the rendering preserves its deceptive plausibility."}, {"source_quote": "τὸ τῆς θεοσεβείας οὐκ ἠδίκει μέρος", "chosen": "did not harm the cause of reverence toward God", "rationale": "μέρος literally denotes the part or sphere belonging to θεοσέβεια; “cause” gives its contextual institutional sense without importing ecclesiastical terminology."}], "source_issues": [{"source_quote": "εἰκόνα φαντασθέντες ἀληθείας· ἣ γὰρ ὄντως οὖσα τότε παρὰ τῷ πρωτίστῳ ὑπῆρχεν θεῷ", "issue": "The feminine relative ἣ can be heard as resuming Helen, while its proximity to ἀληθείας permits a reference to truth or reality. The translation takes it as the real Helen, in contrast with her image; “for the truth, which really existed, was then with the very first God” remains possible.", "proposed_reading": "", "blocks_publication": false}]}, {"reference": "2.26", "text": "For he even began committing murder, as he himself disclosed to friends while he was still a friend: he had separated a child’s soul from its own body by secret oaths, to serve as an assistant in manifesting whatever he pleased; and after depicting the child in a picture, he keeps the picture set up in an inner room where he himself sleeps. He claims that he once formed him out of air through divine transformations, delineated his shape, and then returned him to the air. He explains that he carried out the operation in this way: first, he says, the human spirit, after being changed into the nature of heat, compresses the air surrounding it, drawing it in like a cupping vessel; then it turns the air into water once that air has come inside the form of the spirit. Next, he said, it changes the air within it—which cannot be poured out because of the spirit’s cohesion—into the nature of blood; the blood, upon coagulating, makes flesh; and thus, once the flesh has solidified, it brings forth a human being not from earth but from air. Having thus convinced himself that he could make a new human being, he said that, by undoing the transformations, he had returned him to the air again. While telling these things to others, he was believed, but we who were present with him at the rite piously disbelieved him. Therefore, having condemned him for impiety, we left him.", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "μιαιφονεῖν", "chosen": "committing murder", "rationale": "The verb literally evokes blood-defilement but contextually denotes murderous bloodshed."}, {"source_quote": "ἀπορρήτοις ὅρκοις συνεργὸν πρὸς τὴν τῶν αὐτῷ δοκούντων φαντασίαν", "chosen": "by secret oaths, to serve as an assistant in manifesting whatever he pleased", "rationale": "συνεργόν presents the separated soul as Simon’s assistant, while φαντασία denotes producing an appearance or manifestation. The attachment of “by secret oaths” is compressed and could instead qualify how the soul was made his assistant."}, {"source_quote": "διαγράψας ἐπὶ εἰκόνος", "chosen": "depicting the child in a picture", "rationale": "διαγράφω denotes drawing or delineating, and the subsequent feminine participle points back to the implicitly understood picture."}, {"source_quote": "συνπεῖν ἀέρα", "chosen": "compresses the air", "rationale": "The uncommon verb describes pressing or condensing the surrounding air after drawing it inward."}, {"source_quote": "σικύας δίκην ἐπισπασάμενον", "chosen": "drawing it in like a cupping vessel", "rationale": "A σικύα is a medical cupping vessel that draws material inward by suction."}, {"source_quote": "ὑπὸ δὲ τῆς συνεχείας τοῦ πνεύματος χυθῆναι μὴ δυνάμενον", "chosen": "which cannot be poured out because of the spirit’s cohesion", "rationale": "συνέχεια may denote continuity, cohesion, or enclosing constraint; here it explains why the air cannot flow outward."}, {"source_quote": "ἐπὶ τελετῇ συμπαρόντων", "chosen": "were present with him at the rite", "rationale": "τελετή can denote a rite or an initiation; “rite” avoids deciding whether a formal initiation is specifically meant."}], "source_issues": [{"source_quote": "παιδίου ψυχὴν τοῦ ἰδίου σώματος χωρίσας, ἀπορρήτοις ὅρκοις συνεργὸν πρὸς τὴν τῶν αὐτῷ δοκούντων φαντασίαν", "issue": "The construction is compressed: no separate verb meaning “make” or “bind” is printed with συνεργόν, and ἀπορρήτοις ὅρκοις may attach either to the soul’s separation or to its service as an assistant. The translation treats συνεργόν as the resulting function of the separated soul and attaches the oaths to the separation.", "proposed_reading": "", "blocks_publication": false}, {"source_quote": "εἶτα ἔνδοθεν τῆς τοῦ πνεύματος ἰδέας γενόμενον αὐτὸν τρέψαι εἰς ὕδωρ. ὑπὸ δὲ τῆς συνεχείας τοῦ πνεύματος χυθῆναι μὴ δυνάμενον εἰς αἵματος φύσιν μετατρέπειν ἔφασκεν τὸν ἐν αὐτῷ ἀέρα", "issue": "The sequence leaves the subject of τρέψαι and μετατρέπειν implicit while explicitly naming the air as their object. The translation continues “the spirit” as the agent; an impersonal description of the process is also possible.", "proposed_reading": "", "blocks_publication": false}]}, {"reference": "2.27", "text": "After Aquila said these things, his brother Nicetas said, “It is necessary, our brother Clement, for me to recall what Aquila omitted. First, God is witness that we did not collaborate with him in anything impious, but that we observed what he did. As long as he showed himself doing harmless things, we too were entertained; but when he said that he did by divinity the things that are done by magic, in order to deceive those who revere God, we no longer tolerated him, although he promised us many things: first, that statues of us would be judged worthy of temples, that we would be regarded as gods, worshiped by crowds, glorified by kings, judged worthy of public honors, and enriched with boundless wealth.”", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "τὰ παραλειφθέντα τῷ Ἀκύλᾳ", "chosen": "what Aquila omitted", "rationale": "The dative identifies Aquila as the person responsible for the omission; “what was left for Aquila” is grammatically possible but does not fit Nicetas’s supplementation as well."}, {"source_quote": "οὐδὲν αὐτῷ ἡμεῖς συνειργασάμεθα ἀσεβές", "chosen": "we did not collaborate with him in anything impious", "rationale": "The verb denotes working jointly with someone, while ἀσεβές supplies the impious object or result denied."}, {"source_quote": "αὐτοῦ πράσοντος ἱστορήσαμεν", "chosen": "we observed what he did", "rationale": "ἱστορέω can mean investigating, learning by inquiry, or observing. Here it contrasts witnessing Simon’s actions with participating in them."}, {"source_quote": "τὰ μαγείᾳ γινόμενα θειότητι ποιεῖν", "chosen": "he did by divinity the things that are done by magic", "rationale": "The wording deliberately contrasts the actual means, magic, with Simon’s claimed means, divine power."}, {"source_quote": "ναῶν ἀνδριάντας ἡμῶν καταξιωθῆναι", "chosen": "statues of us would be judged worthy of temples", "rationale": "The unusual promise is that their statues would merit placement or honor in temples; the rendering preserves the source’s genitive construction."}, {"source_quote": "χρήμασιν ἀπεριορίστοις πλουτῆσαι", "chosen": "enriched with boundless wealth", "rationale": "ἀπεριόριστος denotes what is unlimited or without bounds."}], "source_issues": []}, {"reference": "2.28", "text": "“He promised us both these things and the things reckoned with them, on the sole condition that we stay with him and keep silent about the evil of his undertaking, so that the business of deception might prosper for him. Yet we did not agree. Instead, we even advised him to cease from such folly, saying to him, ‘Simon, remembering our friendship with you from childhood and caring for you, we advise you of what is beneficial: Stop such audacity. You cannot be a god. Fear the God who truly is. Know that you are a human being and that your span of life is short. Even if you possess great wealth or even become king, in the short span of your life little is available for enjoyment; and what is acquired impiously, though it immediately vanishes, secures eternal punishment for the one who has dared it. Therefore we advise you to fear God, by whom each person’s soul is to be judged for what it did here.’”", "lexical_decisions": [{"source_quote": "τὰ τούτοις νομιζόμενα", "chosen": "the things reckoned with them", "rationale": "The phrase is compressed and literally concerns things regarded or reckoned in relation to the preceding promises. “Things considered comparable to these” and “similar promises” are viable contextual alternatives."}, {"source_quote": "μόνον ἵνα συνόντες αὐτῷ τὸ τῆς ἐγχειρήσεως κακὸν σιωπῶμεν", "chosen": "on the sole condition that we stay with him and keep silent about the evil of his undertaking", "rationale": "μόνον ἵνα introduces the condition attached to Simon’s promises, while συνόντες denotes continuing in his company."}, {"source_quote": "ἵνα αὐτῷ τὰ τῆς ἀπάτης προκόπτῃ", "chosen": "so that the business of deception might prosper for him", "rationale": "The neuter plural expression denotes the affairs or enterprise of deception; its singular verb is natural with a neuter plural subject."}, {"source_quote": "τῆς τοιαύτης ἀπονοίας", "chosen": "such folly", "rationale": "ἀπόνοια can denote madness, desperation, or reckless folly; the context emphasizes Simon’s irrationally audacious course."}, {"source_quote": "κἂν μεγάλα πλουτῇς ἢ καὶ βασιλεύσῃς", "chosen": "Even if you possess great wealth or even become king", "rationale": "μεγάλα πλουτεῖν describes being wealthy in great possessions, while βασιλεύειν means reigning or holding kingship."}, {"source_quote": "τῷ τῆς ζωῆς σου μικρῷ χρόνῳ ὀλίγα τυγχάνει πρὸς ἀπόλαυσιν", "chosen": "in the short span of your life little is available for enjoyment", "rationale": "The clause stresses that even great wealth or royal power provides only limited enjoyment during a short life."}, {"source_quote": "ἀσεβῶς πορισθέντα, εὐθέως φεύγοντα, αἰωνίαν κόλασιν περιποιεῖται τῷ τετολμηκότι", "chosen": "what is acquired impiously, though it immediately vanishes, secures eternal punishment for the one who has dared it", "rationale": "The substantival participles describe ill-gotten gains as transient yet productive of lasting punishment. The object of “has dared” remains implicit in Greek and is represented by “it.”"}], "source_issues": [{"source_quote": "ταῦτά τε καὶ τὰ τούτοις νομιζόμενα", "issue": "The relation expressed by the dative τούτοις with νομιζόμενα is semantically compressed. The main rendering takes it as things reckoned together with the preceding promises; “things regarded as comparable to these” or more idiomatically “similar promises” is also defensible. No alteration of the printed text is required.", "proposed_reading": "", "blocks_publication": false}]}]}
